WEBVTT

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.895
Welcome to CSC4801, AI-assisted Software Engineering.

00:00:07.895 --> 00:00:11.100
This is Lecture Six: Requirements.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.272
So far, we have built agents.

00:00:14.272 --> 00:00:17.380
And an agent is great at toy projects.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.086
Type one line, like: implement a 2048 game.

00:00:21.086 --> 00:00:24.599
A few minutes later, you have a working game.

00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:28.970
This style is called vibe coding, and it really is cool.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:36.568
But what about a real-world system, with many users, many features, and years of changes?

00:00:36.568 --> 00:00:38.360
How do we handle that?

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:45.439
To get from a toy project to a real system, an agent needs more than a prompt.

00:00:45.439 --> 00:00:47.950
It needs software engineering.

00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:53.950
AI-assisted software engineering covers the whole life of a piece of software.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.893
In this course, we follow it in five stages.

00:00:57.243 --> 00:01:00.776
Requirement engineering decides what to build.

00:01:00.776 --> 00:01:02.082
Coding builds it.

00:01:02.082 --> 00:01:04.693
Testing and verification check it.

00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:06.843
Repair fixes what is broken.

00:01:06.843 --> 00:01:10.683
And AI ops keeps the system running in production.

00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:16.286
Today we start at the very beginning: requirement engineering.

00:01:16.286 --> 00:01:21.793
If this stage is wrong, every later stage builds the wrong thing.

00:01:22.566 --> 00:01:25.635
Requirement engineering is not one step.

00:01:25.635 --> 00:01:28.166
It is a cycle of five activities.

00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:31.370
Elicitation finds out what people need.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:35.174
Modeling turns those needs into a precise structure.

00:01:35.174 --> 00:01:37.443
Specification writes them down.

00:01:37.443 --> 00:01:39.125
Validation checks them.

00:01:39.125 --> 00:01:43.076
And management keeps them up to date as things change.

00:01:43.426 --> 00:01:47.516
It is a cycle because requirements are never finished.

00:01:47.516 --> 00:01:51.226
Management feeds new needs back into elicitation.

00:01:51.576 --> 00:01:56.829
We will use one running example from the slides: an elevator control system.

00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:00.354
And for each activity, we'll ask the same question.

00:02:00.354 --> 00:02:01.736
What can AI do here?

00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:05.413
Let's start with elicitation.

00:02:05.763 --> 00:02:07.811
Real needs are often hidden.

00:02:07.811 --> 00:02:12.198
They are buried in meeting transcripts, and in user reviews.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:15.123
Nobody writes them down as a clean list.

00:02:15.473 --> 00:02:21.701
An AI model can read that unstructured text, and extract latent needs:

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:25.793
needs that users have, but don't say out loud.

00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:28.534
We can go one step further.

00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:35.033
Instead of only reading what real users said, we can use agents to simulate users.

00:02:35.383 --> 00:02:37.765
One research framework works like this.

00:02:37.765 --> 00:02:43.383
First, it generates many user agents, with diversity sampling so they don't all think alike.

00:02:43.733 --> 00:02:49.413
Each agent then imagines using the product, and writes down its experience step by step.

00:02:49.763 --> 00:02:56.243
Next, an interviewer agent asks every user agent a set of questions, and records the answers.

00:02:56.593 --> 00:03:03.873
Finally, the answers are analyzed to identify latent needs, and the result is a report.

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:08.427
Here is how that looks for our elevator control system.

00:03:08.777 --> 00:03:15.170
We create agents that play different users: children, the aged, workers, and managers.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.177
Then we interview each one.

00:03:17.527 --> 00:03:20.247
A child may not reach the top buttons.

00:03:20.247 --> 00:03:24.184
An older person may need the doors to stay open longer.

00:03:24.184 --> 00:03:26.402
A worker may carry heavy loads.

00:03:26.402 --> 00:03:29.767
And a manager may care about energy and safety.

00:03:30.117 --> 00:03:35.197
From these interviews, we collect a list of requirements, written in natural language.

00:03:35.547 --> 00:03:46.747
For example, requirement one: when the user presses the up button on a floor, the elevator shall serve that floor, moving up if necessary, and open its doors on arrival.

00:03:47.097 --> 00:04:00.777
And requirement four: when the overload sensor detects too much weight, the elevator shall prevent further entry, sound an alarm, show a warning, and not move until the weight is reduced.

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:03.931
The second activity is modeling.

00:04:03.931 --> 00:04:14.127
Here, AI identifies the entities and the relationships between them, and builds a model, such as a UML diagram, or a formal specification.

00:04:14.477 --> 00:04:20.797
So the question is: how do we translate a conversation into a specification?

00:04:21.347 --> 00:04:25.158
The conversation is our list of elevator requirements.

00:04:25.158 --> 00:04:31.227
An agent reads it, and produces a specification precise enough for a machine to check.

00:04:31.577 --> 00:04:36.125
One popular target language is linear temporal logic, or LTL.

00:04:36.125 --> 00:04:40.897
It is a way to write rules about how a system behaves over time.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.362
Think of a traffic light.

00:04:43.362 --> 00:04:47.169
Ordinary logic can say: the light is red now.

00:04:47.169 --> 00:04:48.607
LTL can say more.

00:04:48.957 --> 00:04:51.317
The letter G means always.

00:04:51.317 --> 00:04:58.397
G red, then something, means: whenever the light is red, that something holds.

00:04:58.747 --> 00:05:00.877
The letter F means eventually.

00:05:00.877 --> 00:05:04.427
F green means: the light will eventually be green.

00:05:04.777 --> 00:05:07.172
And the letter X means next.

00:05:07.172 --> 00:05:14.617
G, red implies X green, means: whenever it's red, it turns green in the very next step.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:23.440
Writing LTL by hand is hard, so a tool called nl2spec lets an LLM do the translation.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.779
It decomposes the sentence into small pieces.

00:05:27.779 --> 00:05:34.870
Each phrase is translated on its own, such as globally into G, and until into U.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:42.542
Then the user confirms or edits each piece, before they are joined into one final formula.

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:44.820
The human stays in the loop.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.629
Once we have a formula, a model checker can use it.

00:05:48.629 --> 00:05:50.580
We give the checker two things.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:57.917
First, a model of the system: its possible states, and the transitions between them.

00:05:57.917 --> 00:06:03.240
Second, an LTL property, such as: G, request implies F response.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.650
Every request eventually gets a response.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.586
The checker explores the model's possible executions.

00:06:11.586 --> 00:06:20.240
If the property can fail, it returns a counterexample: an execution that shows exactly how it fails.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:21.981
Pause and think.

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:25.262
How would you write this elevator rule in LTL?

00:06:25.262 --> 00:06:31.040
Whenever a floor button is pressed, the doors will eventually open at that floor.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:35.477
One answer: G, pressed implies F open.

00:06:35.477 --> 00:06:39.670
Always, if pressed, then eventually open.

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:43.442
Another target for modeling is Lean 4.

00:06:43.442 --> 00:06:47.747
Lean 4 is a programming language and a proof assistant.

00:06:48.097 --> 00:06:53.400
You write a mathematical statement, or a software specification, and then a proof.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.857
Lean checks that the proof is correct.

00:06:56.207 --> 00:06:59.299
Take a tiny function, max of a and b.

00:06:59.299 --> 00:07:03.727
If a is greater than b, return a, otherwise return b.

00:07:04.077 --> 00:07:11.157
In plain words, the specification is: the result must be at least as large as both inputs.

00:07:11.507 --> 00:07:16.640
In Lean, the specification is a definition with three conditions.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.614
The result is at least a.

00:07:18.614 --> 00:07:20.588
The result is at least b.

00:07:20.588 --> 00:07:23.747
And the result is one of the two inputs.

00:07:24.097 --> 00:07:25.918
Why the third condition?

00:07:25.918 --> 00:07:30.774
Without it, a function that always returns a million would pass.

00:07:30.774 --> 00:07:33.657
The spec must say what we really mean.

00:07:34.207 --> 00:07:37.416
Translating English into Lean is hard too.

00:07:37.416 --> 00:07:41.694
A method called NL2Lean trains an LLM translator for it.

00:07:41.694 --> 00:07:50.327
Several reward models score each Lean statement, and their combined reward is fed back to improve the translator.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:53.828
The third activity is specification.

00:07:53.828 --> 00:07:57.540
The requirements are now decided, and documented.

00:07:57.890 --> 00:07:59.061
The pipeline grows.

00:07:59.061 --> 00:08:07.130
The conversation goes to an agent, the agent writes specifications, and the implementation is checked against those specifications.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.792
The simplest approach is to call an LLM directly.

00:08:10.792 --> 00:08:16.469
We give it the code, and ask it to write a specification as annotations in the code.

00:08:16.469 --> 00:08:19.240
Here, for Java, in a language called JML.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.473
Then a verifier checks the specification against the code.

00:08:23.473 --> 00:08:29.430
If verification fails, we send the error back: these lines failed, please generate again.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.844
A method called SpecGen builds on this.

00:08:32.844 --> 00:08:40.700
First, it has a conversation with the LLM: generate, verify, and send back the errors, a few rounds.

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:44.225
If that still fails, it switches to mutation.

00:08:44.225 --> 00:08:51.210
It makes many small changes to each candidate, selects the promising ones, and verifies them again.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.738
The fourth activity is validation.

00:08:54.738 --> 00:08:58.200
Here, AI scores requirements against rules.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.115
Go back to our elevator.

00:09:00.115 --> 00:09:05.524
Requirement three says the elevator shall serve any floor button pressed inside it.

00:09:05.524 --> 00:09:11.390
Requirement five says, in the overload state, the elevator shall ignore that button press.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.985
So, is there a conflict here?

00:09:13.985 --> 00:09:19.712
An LLM can read the whole list and check the requirements for consistency.

00:09:19.712 --> 00:09:22.420
One study tested this with ChatGPT.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:26.412
Another study compared LLMs with human experts.

00:09:26.412 --> 00:09:34.060
They rated requirements documents on qualities like: unambiguous, understandable, correct, and verifiable.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.170
GPT-4's ratings were comparable to the human ratings.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:41.759
In industry, these ideas form a full pipeline.

00:09:41.759 --> 00:09:48.025
One toolchain for automotive requirements turns natural language into structured English,

00:09:48.025 --> 00:09:52.320
then into a formal timed logic, and runs a consistency check.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:57.359
The inconsistent requirements go to a human, who evaluates them,

00:09:57.359 --> 00:09:59.630
and improves the original text.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.840
The 5th activity is management.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:07.720
Here, AI refines and adapts requirements as the world changes.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:14.045
For example, when a law is revised, the requirements built on it must change too.

00:10:14.045 --> 00:10:19.430
One method compares the old and new versions of a legal text in 4 layers.

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:25.258
After pre-processing, an LLM identifies textual changes: what words changed.

00:10:25.258 --> 00:10:28.646
Then semantic changes: what the meaning now is.

00:10:28.646 --> 00:10:33.980
And finally, deontic changes: what is now required, allowed, or forbidden.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:38.530
Management also means tracking dependencies across features.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.770
One system builds a feature map.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.958
A business analyst agent and an architect agent design the overall model.

00:10:45.958 --> 00:10:50.266
A feature extractor and a feature planner then list the features,

00:10:50.266 --> 00:10:52.320
and the order to build them in.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:58.188
For a timer app, the map links create a timer, start, pause, resume, and history.

00:10:58.188 --> 00:11:02.070
Programmer agents then build the app, feature by feature.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:09.540
And that closes the cycle: elicitation, modeling, specification, validation, and management.

00:11:10.334 --> 00:11:13.534
Now, what are the strengths and weaknesses of all this?

00:11:14.084 --> 00:11:15.379
Here are 2 views.

00:11:15.379 --> 00:11:22.084
One says: in AI-assisted software engineering, requirements are the most important part.

00:11:22.434 --> 00:11:25.698
The other says: we don't need requirements anymore,

00:11:25.698 --> 00:11:28.194
because code can be generated directly.

00:11:28.544 --> 00:11:30.937
Let's look at the strengths first.

00:11:30.937 --> 00:11:33.824
Requirements make us think before we act.

00:11:34.174 --> 00:11:38.136
They put us inside a proper software engineering life cycle,

00:11:38.136 --> 00:11:39.854
instead of one big prompt.

00:11:40.204 --> 00:11:42.342
And they navigate AI agents.

00:11:42.342 --> 00:11:48.604
A clear requirement tells the agent where to go, and tells us when it has arrived.

00:11:49.401 --> 00:11:51.080
Now the weaknesses.

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.881
First, informal language creates ambiguity.

00:11:55.231 --> 00:12:00.751
Consider this requirement: insufficient user balance, reject transactions.

00:12:01.101 --> 00:12:03.215
What does insufficient mean?

00:12:03.215 --> 00:12:08.122
Is the balance less than the amount, or less than or equal to it?

00:12:08.122 --> 00:12:12.501
An agent has to guess, and 2 agents may guess differently.

00:12:13.051 --> 00:12:15.141
Second, requirements change.

00:12:15.141 --> 00:12:19.769
In the classic waterfall model, we analyze requirements first,

00:12:19.769 --> 00:12:22.531
then design, code, test, and deliver.

00:12:22.881 --> 00:12:25.583
But in practice, we keep going back.

00:12:25.583 --> 00:12:29.561
We need to revise the specifications again and again.

00:12:30.161 --> 00:12:32.430
And that leads to the last weakness:

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:36.401
a lot of work switching between writing specs and writing code.

00:12:36.751 --> 00:12:42.191
A spec-driven project can have a requirements file, an API contract,

00:12:42.191 --> 00:12:45.871
a data model, a plan, a spec, and a task list.

00:12:45.871 --> 00:12:50.031
Every code change may mean updating several of them.

00:12:50.801 --> 00:12:52.041
Let's sum up.

00:12:52.391 --> 00:12:55.244
Vibe coding is fine for a toy project.

00:12:55.244 --> 00:13:00.951
But real systems need software engineering, and it starts with requirements.

00:13:01.301 --> 00:13:07.210
Requirement engineering is a cycle: elicitation, modeling, specification,

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:09.396
validation, and management.

00:13:09.396 --> 00:13:11.501
AI can help at every step.

00:13:11.851 --> 00:13:17.496
Formal languages, such as LTL and Lean 4, make requirements precise enough

00:13:17.496 --> 00:13:19.251
for a machine to check.

00:13:19.601 --> 00:13:24.403
But natural language is ambiguous, and requirements keep changing,

00:13:24.403 --> 00:13:28.041
so keeping specs and code in step takes real work.

00:13:28.391 --> 00:13:30.125
Here's something to try.

00:13:30.125 --> 00:13:35.112
Give an LLM the 5 elevator requirements, and ask it to find conflicts

00:13:35.112 --> 00:13:36.558
and ambiguous words.

00:13:36.558 --> 00:13:38.871
Do you agree with what it finds?

00:13:39.668 --> 00:13:41.348
That's all for this lecture.

00:13:41.348 --> 00:13:42.728
Thank you for watching.

00:13:42.728 --> 00:13:47.468
In the next class, Requirements Exercise, you'll put these ideas into practice.

